    "https://unf.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Jacksonville-FL/Senior-Director-of-Student-Success_JR103532": {
      "title": "Senior Director of Student Success",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-01\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nDepartment Student Success Compensation $90,000.00 to $95000.00 Annual   General Description / Primary Purpose The Senior Director of Student Success, under the direction of the Associate Vice President or above, provides strategic, university-wide leadership for academic advising and related student success initiatives within the Office of Student Success. The position is responsible for leading the alignment, coordination, and continuous improvement of academic advising across colleges and advising units, with a focus on advancing best practices that support student learning, retention, progression, and timely graduation. The Senior Director provides direct supervision of Academic Advising leadership including First Year Advising and six colleges. Provides strategic leadership, direction, and accountability for academic advising leaders across the university, establishing clear expectations for performance, student-centered service delivery, collaboration, and progress toward institutional student success goals.  The Senior Director works collaboratively with academic deans, chairs, faculty, Enrollment Services, Student Academic Success Services, learning centers, and other university partners to strengthen advising practices, streamline student support processes, and promote a consistent, proactive, and student-centered advising experience across the institution. The position plays a central role in connecting academic advising, student success programming, transition support, and institutional retention strategies. Job Function The Senior Director provides strategic leadership for university-wide academic advising and student success initiatives within the Office of Student Success. Responsibilities include the following:   Strategic Leadership and Institutional Alignment Provides strategic leadership for the development, implementation, and continuous improvement of a university-wide academic advising strategy aligned with institutional student success priorities. Leads the coordination of advising efforts across colleges and programs, establishing consistent expectations, standards, and best practices that promote student retention, progression, academic planning, and degree completion. Collaborates with academic and administrative leaders to align advising practices, student support services, and institutional initiatives in support of student success.   Organizational Leadership, Talent Development, and Accountability Provides leadership, direction, and accountability for academic advising leaders across the university, establishing clear expectations for performance, service delivery, collaboration, and progress toward institutional student success goals. Builds the leadership capacity of advising directors through coaching, mentorship, performance feedback, professional development, and shared accountability for results. Fosters a high-performing leadership team grounded in trust, collaboration, continuous improvement, innovation, and data-informed decision making. Promotes a culture of leadership accountability and collective ownership of student success outcomes across advising units. Leads a culture in which advising leaders share responsibility for advancing student retention, progression, academic planning, degree completion, and the overall success of the university.   Student Success Strategy, Assessment, and Continuous Improvement  Leads the development, implementation, assessment, and refinement of student success initiatives designed to improve student learning, persistence, retention, and graduation outcomes. Utilizes institutional data, performance metrics, and assessment results to evaluate advising effectiveness, identify barriers to student progression, and inform strategic decision making. Develops partnerships across academic and student affairs units to integrate advising, academic support, First Year Experience, transfer transition, and related student success initiatives into a coordinated support network.   Institutional Partnership, Governance, and Policy Leadership Serves as a key institutional leader and partner in advancing student success initiatives across the university. Collaborates with deans, faculty, department chairs, enrollment leaders, and student affairs professionals to develop and implement strategies that support student achievement. Participates in university committees, councils, task forces, and advisory groups, and contributes to the development, interpretation, and revision of academic policies, procedures, and practices that impact student success. Provides leadership and subject matter expertise related to academic advising, student success, retention, and progression initiatives that influence institutional planning and decision making.   Servant Leadership and Student Success Culture Demonstrates servant leadership through visible engagement with students and staff, modeling a commitment to student-centered problem solving, operational excellence, and responsive service. Maintains an active presence during peak advising and registration periods, partnering with advising teams to address emerging student needs, remove barriers to progression, and ensure effective service delivery. Fosters a culture of accountability, collaboration, and continuous improvement that supports the success of both students and staff. Marginal Functions Contribute to communication, marketing, and fundraising efforts that support the Office of Student Success, academic advising, and student success initiatives. Attend and present at regional and national conferences related to academic advising, student success, retention, progression, and degree completion. Participate in professional organizations and benchmarking activities to remain current on emerging practices in academic advising and student success.   Required Qualifications Master's and 8+ years of relevant experience or Bachelor's degree and 10+ years of relevant experience. Or an equivalent combination of education and experience pursuant to Fla. Stat. 112.219. Departmental Requirements Knowledge of academic advising best practices, student success strategies, retention and progression factors, and issues affecting undergraduate degree completion. Experience in academic advising, student success, academic support, or a closely related area. Demonstrated ability to lead cross-functional partnerships, use data to inform decision-making, assess program effectiveness, and develop strategies that improve student outcomes. Ability to work collaboratively with academic colleges, faculty, administrators, advisors, student support units, and other university stakeholders. Requires a positive, self-motivated leader with excellent interpersonal, organizational, analytical, and communication skills who can lead complex initiatives, build consensus, and advance a coordinated university-wide approach to academic advising and student success. UNF's Student Success team offers a collegial work culture where team members contribute every day to helping students succeed. Your day-to-day efforts will emphasize leading self-directed and autonomous work among your dedicated teams. In addition to being part of a dynamic team, you will also have access to a competitive benefits package https://www.unf.edu/hr/benefits/index.html including UNF's Employee Education Program, which includes up to 6 hours of tuition-free courses each semester. In addition to professional development opportunities in this role, you will have access to UNF's 75 graduate programs in arts and sciences, business, computing and engineering, education, and health. Apply today to become part of a team that empowers students with knowledge, resources, and opportunities to succeed.   Statement(s) of Understanding This position requires a background check. In conjunction with the University's policy, this position may also require a credit check. The holder of this position is designated as a “Responsible Employee” pursuant to their role under Title IX. Therefore, the incumbent must promptly report allegations of sexual misconduct, sexual violence, and child sexual abuse by or against any student, employee, contractor, or visitor to the University’s Title IX Administrator or any divisional Title IX Coordinator     Equal Opportunity The University of North Florida (UNF) is committed to providing an inclusive and welcoming environment for all who interact in our community. In building this environment, we strive to attract students, faculty and staff from a variety of cultures, backgrounds and life experiences. The University of North Florida does not commit or permit discrimination or harassment on the basis of genetic information, race, color, religion, age, sex, disability, marital status, national origin, or veteran status in any educational, employment, social, recreational program or activity that it offers. In addition, UNF will not commit or permit retaliation against an individual who reports discrimination or harassment or an individual who cooperates in an investigation of an alleged violation of university regulation. Carnegie UNF is a Carnegie Community Engaged Institution. This designation celebrates the University’s collaboration with community partners from the local to the global level. It reflects UNF’s mission to contribute to the public good and prepare educated, engaged citizens."
    },
    "https://unf.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Jacksonville-FL/Academic-Advisor_JR103539": {
      "title": "Academic Advisor",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-01\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nDepartment Coggin College of Business, Advising Compensation $45,000.00 Annual   General Description/Primary Purpose The primary purpose of academic advisor to support and guide students throughout their academic journey. Academic advisors play a crucial role in enhancing student success and ensuring a positive and productive educational experience. Academic Advisors serve as a resource to all prospective and current students within the College (undergraduate, post-baccalaureate, and graduate).Academic advisors are required to be familiar with overall Department of Education and Board of Trustees policies and procedures that govern the matriculation process (e.g., general education, Gordon Rule, transfer admissions requirements, registration processes, class scheduling, graduation, etc.)as well as appropriate student referral sources (e.g., academic support services, career services) to facilitate student success.   Job Function Provide individual and group academic advising across all modalities including in person and via phone and virtual platforms, as appropriate. Assist in limited access admission program, where applicable. Assist in probation and suspension decisions and other scheduled administrative functions associated with the academic calendar as needed. Assist in program reviews and provide college data as needed. Prepare productivity reports. Assist in the development of programs that increase persistence rates of undergraduate students while tracking and enhancing graduation rates. Recommend and provide input on academic policies and procedures that impact student persistence and graduation rates. Assist with NCAA compliance regulations pertaining to advising. Serve as a resource and student liaison to academic department chairpersons, the Dean’s office and other student support service departments on campus (e.g., Enrollment Services, Dean of Students, Graduate School, Career Services, other UNF advising offices). Assist in the development and distribution of marketing materials describing offerings across undergraduate and graduate programs. Represent the College at various recruitment events throughout the year. Develops procedures for performing a variety of complex duties within established guidelines; has considerable freedom while the work is in process and receives instructions on new assignments. Assist director with writing, training, and administering policy and procedures for academic advising. Maintains the confidentiality of student records. Initiates contact with students, faculty, and staff of the university on an “as needed” basis. Contact with parents, potential students, and partners in the community as appropriate.   Marginal Functions May be asked to assist in the development of recruitment plans for undergraduate and graduate programs. May be asked to serve on College or University committees, as appropriate. During declared campus emergencies, this position may be required to perform specific job related duties at a designated off campus location or place of residence. Other duties as assigned     Required Qualifications Bachelor's degree and 1+ year of relevant experience, or an equivalent combination of education and experience pursuant to Fla. Stat. 112.219.  UNF’s   Student Success   team offers a high energy, collegial work culture where team members contribute every day to helping students succeed.   After completing onboarding and training in this   position,   you can be part of a   hybrid team   (mix of on-campus and   in-state   remote work). You will rapidly develop portable, marketable skills, expand connections to other professionals in Higher Education, and enjoy opportunities for mentorship and professional development.   Your day-to-day efforts will emphasize self-directed and autonomous work. In addition to being part of a dynamic team, you will also have access to a competitive benefits package   https://www.unf.edu/hr/benefits/index.html   including UNF’s  Employee Education Program , which includes up to 6 hours of tuition-free courses each semester. In addition to skill specialization and professional development opportunities in this role, you could potentially earn a doctoral degree in Educational Leadership or a master's degree or certificate in one of UNF’s 75 graduate programs in arts and sciences, business, computing and engineering, education, and health. Apply today to become part of a team that empowers students with knowledge, resources, and opportunities to succeed.     Statement(s) of Understanding This position requires a background check. In conjunction with the University's policy, this position may also require a credit check. The holder of this position is designated as a “Responsible Employee” pursuant to their role under Title IX. Therefore, the incumbent must promptly report allegations of sexual misconduct, sexual violence, and child sexual abuse by or against any student, employee, contractor, or visitor to the University’s Title IX Administrator or any divisional Title IX Coordinator     Equal Opportunity The University of North Florida (UNF) is committed to providing an inclusive and welcoming environment for all who interact in our community. In building this environment, we strive to attract students, faculty and staff from a variety of cultures, backgrounds and life experiences. The University of North Florida does not commit or permit discrimination or harassment on the basis of genetic information, race, color, religion, age, sex, disability, marital status, national origin, or veteran status in any educational, employment, social, recreational program or activity that it offers. In addition, UNF will not commit or permit retaliation against an individual who reports discrimination or harassment or an individual who cooperates in an investigation of an alleged violation of university regulation. Carnegie UNF is a Carnegie Community Engaged Institution. This designation celebrates the University’s collaboration with community partners from the local to the global level. It reflects UNF’s mission to contribute to the public good and prepare educated, engaged citizens."
    },
    "https://unf.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Jacksonville-FL/Dean-of-Students_JR103535": {
      "title": "Dean of Students",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-31\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nDepartment Student Engagement & International Affairs Compensation $104,511.75 Annual   General Description / Primary Purpose: The Dean of Students provides strategic leadership for student care services, student conduct and academic integrity, behavioral intervention and threat assessment, Title IX prevention education, conflict resolution, and student development initiatives. The position directs the operations of the Dean of Students Office, oversees programs and services that promote student accountability and campus safety, ensures compliance with applicable laws and university policies, and collaborates with campus stakeholders to address student needs and advance institutional priorities. The position serves as a resource to the university community, provides consultation on complex student issues, and develops recommendations, proposals, and reports for senior leadership to support informed decision-making and strategic initiatives. Job Function: Direct efforts to continuously improve the University community by promoting student development and ensuring compliance with federal and state laws, regulations, and university policies. Provide leadership and oversight for the daily operations of student care services, alternative dispute resolution, educational initiatives, student accountability programming, university conduct, and Title IX prevention efforts. Manage fiscal resources and budget authority for the Dean of Students Office. Oversee departmental communications, website content, informational materials, and statistical reporting for the Dean of Students Office. Provide strategic leadership and oversight for the Office of Fraternity & Sorority Life (OFSL). Maintain and administer the Student Handbook, Student Code of Conduct, and Death and Serious Injury Policy. Serve as the primary administrator for Maxient case management software, including coordinating system communication, access, and support with university departments. Chair the Judicial Hold Committee and Medical Withdrawal Committee and oversee the Student Conduct Board, Care Services Team, CATA Team, and Hazing Prevention Committee. Serve as an active member of the University Clery Committee, Alcohol Biennial Review Committee, Healthy Osprey Committee, Safety Council, Title IX Committee, and other committees as needed. Maintain collaborative partnerships with campus departments and stakeholders, including University Police, Housing & Residence Life, General Counsel, the Counseling Center, Undergraduate Studies, and other student service areas. Serve as the primary point of contact for behavioral threat assessment and management concerns and behavioral or academic concerns affecting students and the university community. Monitor students of concern and campus-wide risk factors and provide crisis management, safety strategies, and educational programming to address student needs and promote campus safety. Serve as a member of the campus administrative response team for university events and incidents. Serve as a primary university point of contact for demonstrations, rallies, protests, and other expressive activities, coordinating with campus partners to support student engagement, policy compliance, and campus safety. Direct Medical Withdrawal Committee case management processes, collaborate with university medical professionals, and render decisions based on final case documentation. Oversee the Supporting Our Students (SOS) Team and coordinate interventions for students exhibiting behaviors of concern. Oversee case management services for students experiencing financial hardship, housing insecurity, homelessness, or involvement with the Florida Department of Children and Families (DCF). Oversee alternative dispute resolution and reflection sessions and serve as a facilitator for resolution-based processes when needed. Oversee the recruitment, selection, training, and curriculum development of the Student Conduct Board. Administer the university conduct system, including academic, behavioral, organizational, and sexual misconduct matters, and collaborate with university partners to ensure consistent student messaging and accountability. Assign disciplinary holds, issue No Contact Orders, and facilitate interim measures in collaboration with appropriate university leadership. Review incident reports, assign cases for investigation or processing, and serve as a hearing officer for complex or high-profile cases as needed. Serve as Deputy Title IX Coordinator for Student Affairs and coordinate support, resources, and assistance for students reporting incidents of sexual misconduct. Ensure the Division of Academic & Student Affairs receives training, education, and guidance related to sexual misconduct reporting and response obligations. Serve as a trained facilitator for informal resolutions and as a decision-maker in sexual misconduct and Title IX matters. Marginal Functions: ·         Complete Dean's Certifications, disciplinary clearances, and conduct verifications. ·         Compile and provide student conduct data for the Annual Security Report. ·         During declared campus emergencies, this position may be required to perform specific job-related duties at a designated off campus location. Supervision Exercised: Supervises the Dean of Students team, including Student Care Services, University Conduct, Programming and Resolution, Office of Fraternity and Sorority Life, administrative staff, students, volunteers, and interns. Supervision Received: Reports to the Associate Vice President of Student Engagement and exercises a high level of independent judgment and decision-making within the scope of assigned authority.   Required Qualifications Master's and 6+ years of relevant experience or Bachelor's degree and 8+ years of relevant experience. Or an equivalent combination of education and experience pursuant to Fla. Stat. 112.219.   Statement(s) of Understanding This position requires a background check. In conjunction with the University's policy, this position may also require a credit check. The holder of this position is designated as a “Responsible Employee” pursuant to their role under Title IX. Therefore, the incumbent must promptly report allegations of sexual misconduct, sexual violence, and child sexual abuse by or against any student, employee, contractor, or visitor to the University’s Title IX Administrator or any divisional Title IX Coordinator     Equal Opportunity The University of North Florida (UNF) is committed to providing an inclusive and welcoming environment for all who interact in our community. In building this environment, we strive to attract students, faculty and staff from a variety of cultures, backgrounds and life experiences. The University of North Florida does not commit or permit discrimination or harassment on the basis of genetic information, race, color, religion, age, sex, disability, marital status, national origin, or veteran status in any educational, employment, social, recreational program or activity that it offers. In addition, UNF will not commit or permit retaliation against an individual who reports discrimination or harassment or an individual who cooperates in an investigation of an alleged violation of university regulation. Carnegie UNF is a Carnegie Community Engaged Institution. This designation celebrates the University’s collaboration with community partners from the local to the global level. It reflects UNF’s mission to contribute to the public good and prepare educated, engaged citizens."
    },
    "https://unf.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Jacksonville-FL/Student-Records-Coordinator_JR103528": {
      "title": "Student Records Coordinator",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-31\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nDepartment Registrar's Office Compensation $45,000.00 Annual   General Description / Primary Purpose The Student Records Coordinator serves as the operational coordinator and subject matter expert for assigned student records and registration functions within the Office of the Registrar and Student Success Operations. The position coordinates complex records and registration processes that require interpretation of university policies, academic regulations, and compliance requirements; evaluates records-related issues and workflow exceptions; and ensures the accurate, consistent, and compliant administration of student academic records. The coordinator provides guidance to students, faculty, staff, and campus partners; analyzes recurring operational and records-related concerns; develops recommendations for resolution and process improvement; and supports the development of procedures, training resources, and quality assurance practices. The role exercises independent judgment in coordinating multi-step workflows, resolving complex issues within established policy frameworks, and collaborating across functional areas to support effective service delivery and continuous improvement of records and registration operations. Job Functions Records, Registration, and Workflow Coordination Coordinate student records and registration operations, including oversight of academic record maintenance, registration processes, and related data management activities. Review, validate, maintain, and troubleshoot student academic records and registration data to ensure accuracy, integrity, and compliance. Coordinate complex records and registration workflows involving multiple stakeholders and monitor operational effectiveness, identifying and resolving discrepancies, delays, and process exceptions. Conduct quality assurance reviews and audits to support data integrity, compliance, and consistent application of records and registration procedures. Analyze workflow trends, recurring issues, and operational gaps; recommend process improvements, procedural updates, and communication enhancements to improve efficiency and service delivery. Policy Interpretation, Campus Support, and Issue Resolution Serve as a subject matter expert for student records, registration, academic documents, and related policies and procedures. Interpret and apply institutional, state, and federal requirements to resolve records and registration issues and provide guidance to students, faculty, staff, advisors, and academic departments. Evaluate records-related inquiries and exceptions, determine appropriate resolutions, and communicate guidance and requirements to stakeholders. Collaborate with campus partners to support consistent application of policies and procedures and resolve complex or sensitive student record matters. Research, document, and prepare recommendations for leadership regarding issues requiring policy review, exception handling, or escalation. Academic Document Administration and Compliance Coordinate the production, review, quality control, and distribution of official academic documents, including transcripts, enrollment verifications, degree certifications, and related records. Ensure academic documents are issued in accordance with institutional standards, FERPA requirements, regulatory requirements, and established office procedures. Review document requests and supporting documentation for completeness, accuracy, and compliance prior to approval, release, or escalation. Coordinate associated administrative and financial processes related to assigned records functions, including reconciliation and documentation activities as applicable. Monitor document-related workflows and recommend improvements to strengthen quality control, consistency, and operational effectiveness. Training, Documentation, and Process Improvement Develop, maintain, and update procedures, reference materials, checklists, training resources, and operational documentation for assigned functions. Provide training, guidance, and knowledge sharing to staff and campus stakeholders regarding records and registration processes and requirements. Support the implementation, testing, and communication of operational and system changes affecting records and registration services. Identify opportunities for process improvement and recommend enhancements that improve efficiency, accuracy, compliance, and customer service. Marginal Functions Works closely with other members of the Registrar's Office team, providing backup assistance with routine work, special projects, peak processing periods, and office-wide initiatives. May monitor and order office supplies or assist with administrative support functions. Provides support in the execution of Commencement ceremonies and other university events as assigned. Performs other duties as assigned.   Required Qualifications Master's or Bachelor's degree and 2+ years of relevant experience, or an equivalent combination of education and experience pursuant to Fla. Stat. 112.219.  Department Requirements   Demonstrated ability to manage multiple priorities while   maintaining   accuracy, confidentiality, and attention to detail.   Effective oral and written communication skills.   Strong customer service and interpersonal skills .   Proficiency   in Microsoft Office applications and related business technologies.   Preferred Qualifications   Experience in higher education, student records, registration, enrollment services, advising, or a related student support function.   Experience working with Ellucian Banner or a comparable student information system.   UNF’s   Student Success   team offers a high energy, collegial work culture where team members contribute every day to helping students succeed.   After completing onboarding and training in this   position,   you can be part of a   hybrid team   (mix of on-campus and   in-state   remote work). You will rapidly develop portable, marketable skills, expand connections to other professionals in Higher Education, and enjoy opportunities for mentorship and professional development.   Your day-to-day efforts will emphasize self-directed and autonomous work. In addition to being part of a dynamic team, you will also have access to a competitive benefits package   https://www.unf.edu/hr/benefits/index.html   including UNF’s  Employee Education Program , which includes up to 6 hours of tuition-free courses each semester. In addition to skill specialization and professional development opportunities in this role, you could potentially earn a doctoral degree in Educational Leadership or a master's degree or certificate in one of UNF’s 75 graduate programs in arts and sciences, business, computing and engineering, education, and health. Apply today to become part of a team that empowers students with knowledge, resources, and opportunities to succeed.     Statement(s) of Understanding This position requires a background check. In conjunction with the University's policy, this position may also require a credit check. The holder of this position is designated as a “Responsible Employee” pursuant to their role under Title IX. Therefore, the incumbent must promptly report allegations of sexual misconduct, sexual violence, and child sexual abuse by or against any student, employee, contractor, or visitor to the University’s Title IX Administrator or any divisional Title IX Coordinator     Equal Opportunity The University of North Florida (UNF) is committed to providing an inclusive and welcoming environment for all who interact in our community. In building this environment, we strive to attract students, faculty and staff from a variety of cultures, backgrounds and life experiences. The University of North Florida does not commit or permit discrimination or harassment on the basis of genetic information, race, color, religion, age, sex, disability, marital status, national origin, or veteran status in any educational, employment, social, recreational program or activity that it offers. In addition, UNF will not commit or permit retaliation against an individual who reports discrimination or harassment or an individual who cooperates in an investigation of an alleged violation of university regulation. Carnegie UNF is a Carnegie Community Engaged Institution. This designation celebrates the University’s collaboration with community partners from the local to the global level. It reflects UNF’s mission to contribute to the public good and prepare educated, engaged citizens."
    },
    "https://unf.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Jacksonville-FL/Groundskeeper_JR103525": {
      "title": "Groundskeeper",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-27\nPosted: Posted 8 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nDepartment Physical Facilities, Grounds Compensation $16.00 Hourly   General Description / Primary Purpose: This is work performing grounds maintenance and landscape installation duties including but not restricted to maintenance of turf, landscape, and irrigation; litter control; and set up for special events. Specific duties will be determined by the supervisor. Job Function: Mowing Weed eating, edging asphalt and cement surfaces Plant bed maintenance Equipment/shop maintenance Landscape construction Special projects/events Participate in special event set ups and other work Assist with campus maintenance emergencies Assist with new utilities installation and maintenance of existing utilities Manages work orders Raking leaves Prescribed burning. This position is designated as a Tier 2 respondent for designated campus emergencies and events. In the event of a declared campus emergency, the position incumbent will make every effort to return to campus when directed. Supervision Received: Close supervision. The incumbent's work is reviewed by the supervisor for accuracy and to ensure instructions are being followed.       Required Qualifications Completion of ninth (9th) grade or an equivalent combination of education and experience pursuant to Fla. Stat. 112.219. Some positions may require possession of a classified driver’s license appropriate to a type of vehicle operated.   Statement(s) of Understanding This position requires a background check. In conjunction with the University's policy, this position may also require a credit check. The holder of this position is designated as a “Responsible Employee” pursuant to their role under Title IX. Therefore, the incumbent must promptly report allegations of sexual misconduct, sexual violence, and child sexual abuse by or against any student, employee, contractor, or visitor to the University’s Title IX Administrator or any divisional