    "https://unf.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Jacksonville-FL/Small-Business-Consultant-OPS_JR103430": {
      "title": "Small Business Consultant OPS",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-04\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment Small Business Development Center (SBDC)-OPS Compensation     Required Qualifications Job Description Job Description General Description The Small Business Consultant will provide general consulting services and report to the Associate Director of the FSBDC at UNF and provide services for the 18-county region from the FSBDC at UNF office location. Job Functions Overview   Deliver consulting, training, and outreach in the 18-county service area.   Ensure timely achievement of program deliverables.   Meet assigned KPI’s annually.   Lead assigned programs within the SBDC.   Work collaboratively with university staff to ensure program’s success. May interpret departmental policies for area of responsibility.   Consultant reports to the UNF SBDC Associate Director.   Employee will provide direct client services including group training, individual counsel, and public appearances. Consulting   Assist small businesses with activities to start and grow a Florida based small business.   Consultant delivers UNF SBDC’s targeted focus programs as assigned, including micro enterprise assistance programs.   Consultant will collaborate and coordinate with   Assist clients in developing and implementing business plans.   Perform financial analysis and create financial projections. Training   Create educational materials and present independent SBDC workshops as well as joint programs and workshops with stakeholders.   Identify opportunities to deliver workshops to potential SBDC clients and new stakeholders. Outreach   Conduct outreach to educate clients, partners, and stakeholders on SBDC services and impact.   Represent the UNF SBDC at community business events including breakfast meetings and after-hours events.   Employee will assist in generating additional grant dollars to expand micro UNF SBDC services in the 18-county service area.   Represent the UNF SBDC on various local Boards as assigned or elected. Supervision / Administration Foster an engaged workforce through effective supervision. Employee will supervise assigned small business consulting coordinators, OPS Consultants, student assistants, and/or training assistants. The Small Business Consultant is responsible for effective administration of FSBDC services in the 18-county service area.   Travel   Travel within the 18-county service area as required.   Attend state and national professional development conferences as assigned.   Ability to attend pre and post business-hours meetings of local business organizations. Marginal Functions During declared campus emergencies, this position may be required to perform specific job-related duties at a designated off campus location or place of residence and other duties as assigned.   Statement(s) of Understanding This position requires a background check. In conjunction with the University's policy, this position may also require a credit check. The holder of this position is designated as a “Responsible Employee” pursuant to their role under Title IX. Therefore, the incumbent must promptly report allegations of sexual misconduct, sexual violence, and child sexual abuse by or against any student, employee, contractor, or visitor to the University’s Title IX Administrator or any divisional Title IX Coordinator     Equal Opportunity The University of North Florida (UNF) is committed to providing an inclusive and welcoming environment for all who interact in our community. In building this environment, we strive to attract students, faculty and staff from a variety of cultures, backgrounds and life experiences. The University of North Florida does not commit or permit discrimination or harassment on the basis of genetic information, race, color, religion, age, sex, disability, marital status, national origin, or veteran status in any educational, employment, social, recreational program or activity that it offers. In addition, UNF will not commit or permit retaliation against an individual who reports discrimination or harassment or an individual who cooperates in an investigation of an alleged violation of university regulation. Carnegie UNF is a Carnegie Community Engaged Institution. This designation celebrates the University’s collaboration with community partners from the local to the global level. It reflects UNF’s mission to contribute to the public good and prepare educated, engaged citizens."
    },
    "https://unf.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Jacksonville-FL/Assistant-Director-Competitive-Sports-and-Camps_JR103406": {
      "title": "Assistant Director Competitive Sports and Camps",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-04\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nDepartment Recreation & Wellness, Field House Compensation $55,000.00 to Negotiable Annual   General Description / Primary Purpose The Assistant Director of Competitive Sports and Camps provides strategic leadership and operational oversight for a year-round Competitive Sports program that includes intramural sports, club sports, open recreation in the Field House, special events and facility rentals, and the Youth Sports and Fitness Camp. Under the direction of an Associate Director or above, this position designs, implements, evaluates, and interprets department processes, policies, and risk management systems so programs are safe, inclusive, fiscally responsible, and aligned with University of North Florida Recreation and Wellness goals. This position supervises two full-time coordinators, student employees, volunteers, interns, and camp staff; manages program budgets, equipment, facilities, records, and web-based systems; collaborates with campus partners, Student Government, Athletics, the RecWell Business Office, and external clients; and uses assessment data, participation trends, and industry best practices to improve programs, staff training, facility use, revenue processes, and student engagement. Job Functions Program Administration and Strategic Leadership ·  Administer a comprehensive Competitive Sports portfolio, including intramurals, club sports, Field House open recreation, special events and facility rentals, and the Youth Sports and Fitness Camp. ·  Set unit vision, goals, service expectations, and annual priorities in support of departmental goals and student engagement outcomes. ·  Develop, implement, interpret, and periodically revise program policies, procedures, manuals, sportsmanship expectations, and operating standards. ·  Support campus and external facility-use requests for RecWell courts, fields, and the Field House, including pricing models, contracts, invoicing, and scheduling systems. ·  Assist with strategies and objectives that strengthen program participation, access, service quality, and operational consistency. Supervision, Training, and Staff Development ·  Supervise two full-time coordinators and provide leadership for Competitive Sports, Field House, special event, and summer camp staffing structures. ·  Provide indirect oversight for more than 60 student employees, including competitive sports supervisors, officials, referees, safety monitors, Field House staff, special event staff, camp staff, and other assigned student or temporary personnel. ·  Recruit, train, schedule, coach, and evaluate staff, students, volunteers, graduate assistants, and interns as assigned. ·  Design and deliver presentations, workshops, official training, onboarding materials, and ongoing staff development resources. ·  Encourage professional engagement and committee service among direct reports and support staff growth through relevant campus and professional development opportunities. Budget, Business Operations, and Systems Management ·  Manage financial decisions within the program scope and support annual and long-term budget planning for the Competitive Sports operations. ·  Work closely with the RecWell Business Office in support of A&S funds, summer camp fees, auxiliary revenue, external and internal client billing, and event-related collections. ·  Maintain a repair and replacement plan for Competitive Sports facilities, equipment, and program needs. ·  Use web-based and desktop systems, including EMS, ActiveNet, IMLeagues, and similar applications, for scheduling, staff records, program administration, billing support, reports, and operational documentation. ·  Maintain clear, current, and accessible web information for the unit and summer camp in partnership with the RecWell Business Office. Operations, Facilities, Equipment, and Risk Management ·  Oversee general operations of the Field House, outdoor sport recreation areas, fields, and Competitive Sports program spaces. ·  Develop and maintain a comprehensive risk management program for Competitive Sports and Camps, including periodic review of systems, manuals, tools, emergency action plans, and best practices. ·  Implement facility and equipment inspections, preventive maintenance checklists, routine reports, and hazard identification processes that support safe operations. ·  Maintain an equipment inventory system that tracks program and facility equipment, anticipated shelf life, replacement timing, and reordering needs. ·  Monitor activities and events in large program areas and identify potential hazards during facility inspections and program delivery. Campus Collaboration and External Relationships ·  Collaborate with the Office of Dean of Students and the Office of Student Accountability and Resolution regarding conduct considerations related to Competitive Sports participation. ·  Coordinate with Athletics to support athletic team needs for RecWell fields and facilities and RecWell-managed events in the Arena. ·  Maintain working relationships with Student Government agencies, Osprey Involvement Center, Osprey Life and Productions, Fraternity and Sorority Life, and other campus partners for cross-collaboration and program engagement. ·  Support Camps on Campus program coordination and maintain professional association involvement with the American Camp Association for summer camp management best practices. ·  Maintain professional association involvement with NIRSA to remain current with Competitive Sports standards, training practices, and program trends. Marketing, Assessment, and Continuous Improvement ·  Develop and support marketing strategies that increase awareness and participation in Competitive Sports, Camps, Field House programs, and related special events. ·  Collaborate with campus marketing and communication resources such as Osprey Update, events calendars, publications, Spinnaker media, and culturally relevant social media tools. ·  Implement participation assessment tools and maintain accurate program statistics, survey data, and usage reports. ·  Prepare program reports for departmental, annual report, and Student Government needs, including analysis of participation, program use, trends, and improvement recommendations. ·  Analyze statistics, surveys, evaluations, and operational data to inform strategic planning recommendations and continuous improvement for the Competitive Sports Unit. Marginal Functions ·  Travel to professional conferences, association meetings, or professional development opportunities as funding and operational needs allow. ·  Maintain professional certifications, memberships, or registry affiliations relevant to Competitive Sports and camp administration. ·  During declared campus emergencies, perform assigned job-related duties at a designated campus, off-campus, or remote location as directed.   Required Qualifications Master's and 2+ years of relevant experience or Bachelor's degree and 4+ years of relevant experience. Or an equivalent combination of education and experience pursuant to Fla. Stat. 112.219. Departmental Requirements Reliability and Schedule: Must be able to work evening and weekend hours based on program, event, and operational needs. Communication: Must communicate effectively with participants, student employees, professional staff, campus partners, families, vendors, and external clients orally and in writing. Communication must be appropriate for staff training, presentations, participant education, customer service, and representation of the program area and department. Visual and Auditory Abilities: Must have visual and auditory abilities sufficient to monitor activities and events in areas measuring approximately 26,000 square feet and to identify potential hazards during facility inspections. Technology and Equipment: Must be proficient with computers and department-relevant web-based applications, including scheduling, registration, billing, records management, reporting, and program operations systems such as EMS, ActiveNet, IMLeagues, and similar applications. Must be able to operate standard office equipment and sport or recreation equipment pertinent to the work area. Professional Standards: Must maintain knowledge of current trends, risk management practices, training standards, and professional resources relevant to Competitive Sports, Camps, facility operations, and student employee development.   Statement(s) of Understanding This position requires a background check. In conjunction with the University's policy, this position may also require a credit check. The holder of this position is designated as a “Responsible Employee” pursuant to their role under Title IX. Therefore, the incumbent must promptly report allegations of sexual misconduct, sexual violence, and child sexual abuse by or against any student, employee, contractor, or visitor to the University’s Title IX Administrator or any divisional Title IX Coordinator   This position has been designated as a Campus Security Authority (CSA). This position has been designated as a CSA because it involves significant responsibility for student and campus activities and/or responsibility for campus security. As such, any person in this position must report to the University Police Department a crime or an incident that might be a crime that he/she becomes aware of. This position has a reporting requirement because the University believes that responsibility for students, campus activities and/or campus security will make the holder of this position someone members of the University community will see as an authority figure and someone to whom they can seek help. A CSA is not expected to investigate or determine whether a criminal incident actually took place. A CSA ’ s responsibility is a duty to report. All positions designated as CSAs are required to complete an online or in - person training session. For more information concerning training or CSA designations, please contact the Clery Act Compliance Manager at (904) 620-1423 or clery@unf.edu.   Equal Opportunity The University of North Florida (UNF) is committed to providing an inclusive and welcoming environment for all who interact in our community. In building this environment, we strive to attract students, faculty and staff from a variety of cultures, backgrounds and life experiences. The University of North Florida does not commit or permit discrimination or harassment on the basis of genetic information, race, color, religion, age, sex, disability, marital status, national origin, or veteran status in any educational, employment, social, recreational program or activity that it offers. In addition, UNF will not commit or permit retaliation against an individual who reports discrimination or harassment or an individual who cooperates in an investigation of an alleged violation of university regulation. Carnegie UNF is a Carnegie Community Engaged Institution. This designation celebrates the University’s collaboration with community partners from the local to the global level. It reflects UNF’s mission to contribute to the public good and prepare educated, engaged citizens."
    },
